window.docContents[74].resolve({"/Development-Server/#serve-behavior":{"contents":"* A file's Content-Type comes from its extension.\n  A file whose extension is unknown is served as text/plain when its contents are valid UTF-8 text, so text-like files open in the browser instead of downloading, and as application/octet-stream otherwise.* Directories are served by their index.html when one is present; otherwise a generated listing is shown unless listings are disabled, in which case the request is refused with 403 Forbidden.* A request for a directory without a trailing slash is redirected to add one, so relative links resolve correctly. This can be turned off.* Range requests are supported, so media files can be sought and large files resumed.* Symbolic links are followed only while their target stays within a mounted directory. A link pointing outside every mount is refused, unless follow_symlinks_outside_root is set.* Only GET and HEAD are served. Other methods receive 405 Method Not Allowed. With CORS enabled, OPTIONS preflight requests are answered.\n\n\n","context":"Writing Documentation in Lean with Verso\u0009Development Server","header":"9.5. Other Behavior","id":"/Development-Server/#serve-behavior"},"/Output-Formats/#output-tex":{"contents":"Verso's TeX type represents LaTeX documents.\nThey are typically produced using an embedded DSL that is available when the namespace Verso.Output.TeX is opened.\n\nTeX outputText to be shown in the document, escaped as needed.Raw TeX code to be included without escaping.A LaTeX command, with the provided optional and mandatory arguments (in square and curly brackets, respectively)A LaTeX environment, with the provided optional and mandatory arguments (in square and curly brackets, respectively)A paragraph break, rendered to TeX as a blank lineConcatenation of TeX\n\nThe empty TeX document\n\nConverts a TeX document to a string to be processed by LaTeX\n\nTeX documents are written in \\TeX{...}, in a syntax very much like LaTeX itself.\nThe differences are:\n\n* \\Lean{...} escapes back to Lean, expecting a value of type TeX.* Text content is written as Lean string literals to facilitate precise control over whitespace.* Interpolated Lean strings (with s!) may be used in any context that expects a string.\n\nFor example, this definition creates a bulleted list list:\n\nopen Verso.Output.TeX\n\ndef mkList (xs : List TeX) : TeX :=\n  \\TeX{\n    \\begin{itemize}\n      \\Lean{xs.map (\\TeX{\\item \" \" \\Lean{·} \"\\n\"})}\n    \\end{itemize}\n  }\n\n#eval mkList [\"A\", \\TeX{\\emph{\"B\"}}, \"C\"]\n  |>.asString\n  |> IO.println\n\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item A\n\\item \\emph{B}\n\\item C\n\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\n","context":"Writing Documentation in Lean with Verso\u0009Output Formats","header":"5.2. TeX","id":"/Output-Formats/#output-tex"}});